Encompasses: Conditioning power purchase agreements on safety audits, tying public funding to adherence to risk management frameworks, linking utility approvals to federal evaluations.
  Vulnerability: Conditioning critical infrastructure and grants on compliance with federal safety standards risks creating a rigid regulatory monopoly that stifles open-source competition, empowers incumbent tech giants who can easily absorb compliance overhead, and paralyzes AI deployment through bureaucratic friction.
★ [saf-intentions-087] (operationality: 5/5)
  "Replacing Abstract Safety Claims with Testable Engineering Controls" — An Intention within safetyist discourse that conditions the deployment of AI systems on testable engineering controls backed by named human authority and mandatory rollback mechanisms. 
Encompasses: system-level constraint verification, named executive accountability, mandatory operational halt authorities, architectural fail-safes.
  Vulnerability: Mandatory operational halt authorities and named executive accountability may incentivize risk-averse corporate jurisdictions to freeze beneficial algorithmic progress or drive critical research underground into unregulated environments.
★ [saf-intentions-069] (operationality: 5/5)
  "Grounding AI Safety in Legal Compliance Rather Than Abstract Ethics" — An Intention within safetyist discourse that prioritizes ensuring AI systems comply with existing legal statutes as the primary basis for safety, rather than abstract ethical values. 
Encompasses: Using established legal frameworks, compliance audits, and legal interpretations to guide AI development and deployment.
  Vulnerability: A legal-compliance-first approach may systematically under-protect against novel AI risks because existing statutes were drafted for prior technological paradigms; by anchoring safety to what is currently codified, this strategy risks institutionalizing a ceiling rather than a floor, allowing harms that are technically legal but genuinely dangerous to proceed unchecked while the slower legislative process catches up.
